response:
Valid concern. The merge isn't free, but it's also not the killer it might sound like — and there's a hybrid that mostly avoids it.

## The merge itself

RocksDB doesn't iterate across CFs natively. You open one iterator per CF and merge in application code:

```cpp
std::vector<rocksdb::ColumnFamilyHandle*> handles = day_handles_in_range(t0, t1);
std::vector<rocksdb::Iterator*> iters;
db->NewIterators(read_opts, handles, &iters);
for (auto* it : iters) it->Seek(start_key);

// Min-heap by key
auto cmp = [](Iterator* a, Iterator* b) {
  return a->key().compare(b->key()) > 0;
};
std::priority_queue<Iterator*, std::vector<Iterator*>, decltype(cmp)> pq(cmp);
for (auto* it : iters) if (it->Valid()) pq.push(it);

while (!pq.empty()) {
  Iterator* top = pq.top(); pq.pop();
  emit(top->key(), top->value());
  top->Next();
  if (top->Valid() && top->key().compare(end_key) <= 0) pq.push(top);
}
```

For a 7-day scan that's a 7-way heap merge. Per emitted record: one heap pop, one Next() on an iterator, one heap push. That's ~50 ns of overhead on top of the actual SST read. Compared to the network and serialization cost of streaming results to a client, it's invisible.

## Why it's usually fine

For 90-day retention with daily CFs, your scans land in one of these buckets:

- "Last hour" / "today" — 1 CF, no merge.
- "Last 24 hours" — 1-2 CFs.
- "Last week" — 7 CFs. 7-way heap merge is trivial.
- "Last 30 days" — 30 CFs. Now you're feeling it; the merge is still O(log 30) per record, ~5x more comparisons, but the dominant cost is reading the data.

The pathological case is cross-everything analytics. Don't do those on the live store; replicate to ClickHouse or Parquet for that.

## The hybrid: weekly CFs

Daily CFs are simple but excessive. Weekly CFs over 90 days: 13 CFs. Almost every query touches 1-2 CFs. Wholesale drops happen weekly instead of daily — fine for retention precision.

Monthly CFs (4 of them) is even less merging but means you keep up to 30 days of "about to be deleted" data. Trade off granularity for simplicity.

## The escape hatch: stop building this yourself

At this point you're hand-rolling what TimescaleDB or InfluxDB does in one config option. If you're not committed to RocksDB-as-library, **TimescaleDB** is worth a serious look:

```sql
CREATE TABLE events (ts timestamptz NOT NULL, payload jsonb);
SELECT create_hypertable('events', 'ts', chunk_time_interval => INTERVAL '1 day');
SELECT add_retention_policy('events', INTERVAL '90 days');
```

That's it. Behind the scenes:
- Automatic chunk-per-day (your CF-per-day, but transparent).
- Chunk exclusion at query time — only relevant chunks get scanned, no app-side merging.
- BRIN-or-btree per chunk for sub-day filtering.
- Optional per-chunk columnar compression after N days, often 10-20x.
- Drop-chunk for retention is the same wholesale-delete trick.
- 50k writes/sec is well within range with proper batching.

TimescaleDB on a beefy box vs RocksDB-from-scratch with daily CFs: same architecture, an order of magnitude less code you maintain.

## Decision tree

- Embedded service, must avoid an external DB, comfortable with C++ ops: **RocksDB + universal compaction + weekly CFs**. Heap-merge across CFs is fine.
- Anything else, especially "we run on Kubernetes and have a Postgres operator": **TimescaleDB**. You'll ship a year sooner.

The heap merge is real but it's not the deciding factor. The deciding factor is whether you want to be in the storage-engine business.

Here's a production-grade scheduler. Validates the graph, emits a precise cycle path on cycles, runs ready tasks up to a concurrency cap, cancels pending work on first failure via `AbortController`:

```typescript
export interface Target {
  name: string;
  deps: string[];
  run: (signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<void>;
}

export interface BuildOptions {
  concurrency: number;
  onStart?: (name: string) => void;
  onFinish?: (name: string, ms: number) => void;
}

export class CycleError extends Error {
  constructor(public readonly path: string[]) {
    super(`cycle: ${path.join(" -> ")}`);
    this.name = "CycleError";
  }
}

export class BuildError extends Error {
  constructor(public readonly target: string, public readonly cause: unknown) {
    super(`target "${target}" failed: ${cause instanceof Error ? cause.message : cause}`);
    this.name = "BuildError";
  }
}

function validate(targets: Target[]): Map<string, Target> {
  const byName = new Map<string, Target>();
  for (const t of targets) {
    if (byName.has(t.name)) throw new Error(`duplicate target: ${t.name}`);
    byName.set(t.name, t);
  }
  for (const t of targets) {
    for (const d of t.deps) {
      if (!byName.has(d)) throw new Error(`target "${t.name}" depends on unknown "${d}"`);
    }
  }
  return byName;
}

/** DFS coloring; throws CycleError with the actual cycle path. */
function findCycle(byName: Map<string, Target>): void {
  const WHITE = 0, GRAY = 1, BLACK = 2;
  const color = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const name of byName.keys()) color.set(name, WHITE);

  const stack: string[] = [];

  function visit(name: string): void {
    const c = color.get(name);
    if (c === BLACK) return;
    if (c === GRAY) {
      const idx = stack.indexOf(name);
      throw new CycleError([...stack.slice(idx), name]);
    }
    color.set(name, GRAY);
    stack.push(name);
    for (const d of byName.get(name)!.deps) visit(d);
    stack.pop();
    color.set(name, BLACK);
  }

  for (const name of byName.keys()) visit(name);
}

export async function runBuild(targets: Target[], opts: BuildOptions): Promise<void> {
  if (opts.concurrency < 1) throw new Error("concurrency must be >= 1");
  const byName = validate(targets);
  findCycle(byName); // fail fast with precise path

  // Build reverse adjacency: when a target finishes, decrement these dependents' indegree.
  const dependents = new Map<string, string[]>();
  const inDegree = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const t of targets) {
    inDegree.set(t.name, t.deps.length);
    if (!dependents.has(t.name)) dependents.set(t.name, []);
    for (const d of t.deps) {
      if (!dependents.has(d)) dependents.set(d, []);
      dependents.get(d)!.push(t.name);
    }
  }

  const ready: string[] = [];
  for (const [name, deg] of inDegree) if (deg === 0) ready.push(name);

  const ctrl = new AbortController();
  let running = 0;
  let completed = 0;
  const total = targets.length;
  let firstError: unknown = null;

  return new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {
    const launch = (): void => {
      if (firstError !== null) {
        if (running === 0) reject(firstError);
        return;
      }
      while (running < opts.concurrency && ready.length > 0) {
        const name = ready.shift()!;
        const target = byName.get(name)!;
        running++;
        opts.onStart?.(name);
        const t0 = performance.now();
        target.run(ctrl.signal).then(
          () => {
            running--;
            completed++;
            opts.onFinish?.(name, performance.now() - t0);
            for (const dep of dependents.get(name) ?? []) {
              const next = (inDegree.get(dep) ?? 0) - 1;
              inDegree.set(dep, next);
              if (next === 0) ready.push(dep);
            }
            if (completed === total) { resolve(); return; }
            launch();
          },
          (err) => {
            running--;
            if (firstError === null) {
              firstError = new BuildError(name, err);
              ctrl.abort();
            }
            launch();
          },
        );
      }
      if (running === 0 && ready.length === 0 && firstError === null && completed < total) {
        // Should be unreachable post-cycle-check, but defensive.
        reject(new Error("scheduler stalled — orphaned dependencies"));
      }
    };
    launch();
  });
}
```

Usage:

```typescript
const targets: Target[] = [
  { name: "compile-a", deps: [], run: async (s) => { await sh("tsc -p a", s); } },
  { name: "compile-b", deps: [], run: async (s) => { await sh("tsc -p b", s); } },
  { name: "link",      deps: ["compile-a", "compile-b"], run: async (s) => { await sh("ld ...", s); } },
  { name: "test",      deps: ["link"], run: async (s) => { await sh("vitest", s); } },
];

try {
  await runBuild(targets, {
    concurrency: 4,
    onStart: (n) => console.log(`> ${n}`),
    onFinish: (n, ms) => console.log(`  ${n} (${ms.toFixed(0)}ms)`),
  });
} catch (e) {
  if (e instanceof CycleError) console.error("build graph has a cycle:", e.path);
  else console.error(e);
  process.exit(1);
}
```

A few things worth knowing:

- **Cycle output is the actual cycle**, not the entire graph. If you have `a -> b -> c -> a` plus 500 unrelated targets, you get exactly `a -> b -> c -> a`. The DFS-with-coloring trick is what makes this work — when you re-enter a GRAY node, slice the recursion stack from that node onward.

- **AbortController is the cancellation contract**. Tasks must honor `signal` — pass it to `child_process.spawn`, `fetch`, etc. Tasks that ignore it will run to completion even after a sibling failed; the build will reject after they drain.

- **Complexity**: Kahn's is O(V + E), the DFS cycle check is O(V + E). For typical build graphs (thousands of targets, tens of thousands of edges) this is sub-millisecond — the wall time is dominated by `target.run()`.

- **What this doesn't handle**: incremental rebuilds (skip up-to-date targets via content hashing — Bazel/Buck style), distributed execution (RPC the `run` to remote workers), and resource-aware scheduling (some targets are CPU-bound, some I/O-bound — give them separate semaphores). Those are the next layer up; the topo scheduler stays the same.

The memory leak is caused by **circular references** between your Node objects. Every edge creates a reference cycle: node A's `neighbors` list holds a reference to node B, and node B's `neighbors` list holds a reference back to node A. These cycles prevent CPython's primary garbage collection mechanism — reference counting — from ever freeing the nodes.

CPython does have a secondary cyclic garbage collector that detects and breaks reference cycles, but it runs based on allocation thresholds and promotes long-lived objects to higher generations that are scanned less frequently. With graphs of 1000 nodes being created rapidly, the cyclic GC may not trigger often enough to keep up, causing memory to climb steadily.

There are several ways to fix this:

**Option 1: Use `weakref` for one direction of the edge**

```python
import weakref

class Node:
    def __init__(self, node_id, data=None):
        self.node_id = node_id
        self.data = data
        self._neighbor_refs = []

    @property
    def neighbors(self):
        return [ref() for ref in self._neighbor_refs if ref() is not None]

    def add_neighbor(self, other):
        self._neighbor_refs.append(weakref.ref(other))
```

**Option 2: Explicitly break cycles when done with a graph**

```python
class Graph:
    def __init__(self):
        self.nodes = {}

    def clear(self):
        """Break all reference cycles so GC can collect nodes."""
        for node in self.nodes.values():
            node.neighbors.clear()
        self.nodes.clear()

# Main loop
for i in range(10000):
    g = Graph()
    build_random_graph(g, num_nodes=1000)
    metrics = process_graph(g)
    save_metrics(metrics)
    g.clear()  # Explicitly break cycles
```

**Option 3: Use an adjacency-list representation without object references**

```python
class Graph:
    def __init__(self):
        self.node_data = {}  # node_id -> data
        self.adj = {}         # node_id -> set of neighbor node_ids

    def add_node(self, node_id, data=None):
        self.node_data[node_id] = data
        self.adj.setdefault(node_id, set())

    def add_edge(self, id_a, id_b):
        self.adj[id_a].add(id_b)
        self.adj[id_b].add(id_a)
```

Option 3 is the best approach for most use cases. By storing node IDs instead of object references, there are no reference cycles at all. The entire graph is just dicts and sets of integers, which are trivially garbage collected. This also tends to be more memory-efficient and faster to serialize.

Depends on which model you're using:

**Single render pass, two subpasses (G-buffer subpass + lighting subpass):** No, you don't need the manual barrier. Declare a `VkSubpassDependency`:

```cpp
VkSubpassDependency dep{};
dep.srcSubpass      = 0;  // gbuffer
dep.dstSubpass      = 1;  // lighting
dep.srcStageMask    = VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_COLOR_ATTACHMENT_OUTPUT_BIT;
dep.dstStageMask    = VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_FRAGMENT_SHADER_BIT;
dep.srcAccessMask   = VK_ACCESS_COLOR_ATTACHMENT_WRITE_BIT;
dep.dstAccessMask   = VK_ACCESS_INPUT_ATTACHMENT_READ_BIT;
dep.dependencyFlags = VK_DEPENDENCY_BY_REGION_BIT;
```

`BY_REGION_BIT` is the magic — it tells the implementation each fragment only depends on the same fragment in the previous subpass, which lets tilers (Mali/Adreno/PowerVR) keep the G-buffer in tile memory and never flush to main memory. Huge win on mobile, basically free on desktop.

**Two separate render passes or dynamic rendering:** Yes, you need the explicit `vkCmdPipelineBarrier`. There's no implicit synchronization across render pass boundaries (other than render-pass-external dependencies, which are equivalent to a barrier).

**Practical note:** Most modern desktop engines have moved to dynamic rendering and abandoned subpasses, because the tile-memory benefit doesn't apply on IMR GPUs and subpasses complicate render graph design. If you're targeting desktop only, dynamic rendering + explicit barriers is cleaner. If you ship on mobile, subpasses are still worth it for the bandwidth savings.

One thing the within-frame barrier *won't* fix and the subpass dependency will: input attachment reads are framebuffer-local. If your lighting pass needs to sample the G-buffer with a non-(0,0) offset (e.g., for blur or screen-space effects), input attachments don't work — you need separate render passes and a real `SHADER_READ` barrier.

Don't denormalize yet. Denormalization is a real tool but it's a big consistency tax and often isn't the right fix for this shape of problem. Climb the diagnostic ladder first.

**Step 1: Look at the actual SQL plan**

A "batched query joining 4 tables" being slow is usually one of:

- Missing index on a foreign key (`comments.post_id`, `comments.author_id`)
- Sequential scan because the planner mis-estimates rows
- Sorts spilling to disk because the result set is large
- A LIMIT being pushed below a join in a way that disables an index

```sql
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) <your batched query>;
```

Look for `Seq Scan` on tables you expected indexed access to, large `Rows Removed by Filter` numbers, and `external merge Disk` for sorts. If you have an unindexed foreign key, adding the index is a 100x win and you stop here.

**Step 2: Reconsider the big join**

The instinct "one big query is faster than many small ones" is wrong as often as it's right at this scale. A 4-way join with filtering and ordering is computationally heavier than four `WHERE id IN (...)` lookups even accounting for round trips, because:

- Each simple `IN` lookup is an index range scan, often index-only.
- The planner can't be confused by stats on simple lookups.
- Working set per query is small, fits in cache, doesn't spill.
- You can parallelize across connections (if you have a healthy pool).

The DataLoader-friendly decomposition for your query:

```ts
const loaders = {
  postsLatest: new DataLoader(async (limits) => {
    // Just fetch posts ordered by recency, no joins
    return Promise.all(limits.map(n =>
      db.post.findMany({ orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' }, take: n })
    ));
  }),
  userById: new DataLoader(batchUsersById),
  commentsByPostId: new DataLoader(batchCommentsByPostId),
};
```

Resolvers stay clean:

```ts
const Post = {
  author: (p, _, { loaders }) => loaders.userById.load(p.authorId),
  comments: (p, _, { loaders }) => loaders.commentsByPostId.load(p.id),
};

const Comment = {
  author: (c, _, { loaders }) => loaders.userById.load(c.authorId),
};
```

GraphQL fires three SQL queries: posts (50 rows), users (~100 IDs deduplicated), comments-by-post (50 keys). Each is index-clean. In my experience this beats the 4-way join in p99 by 30-60% on Postgres at moderate scale.

**Step 3: Lookahead / field projection**

If a client asks for `posts { id title }` you don't need to load author or comments at all. Most ORMs let you build the SELECT dynamically based on requested fields. Libraries like `graphql-parse-resolve-info` give you the AST of the current selection. Cheap, big win — you skip work you'd otherwise do speculatively. The hand-rolled version:

```ts
import { parseResolveInfo } from 'graphql-parse-resolve-info';

const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    posts: async (_, { limit }, ctx, info) => {
      const fields = parseResolveInfo(info);
      const needsAuthor = 'author' in fields.fieldsByTypeName.Post;
      // Conditionally include the join or rely on DataLoader
      // ...
    },
  },
};
```

For more automation, `join-monster` generates a single SQL query from a GraphQL query. Powerful but opinionated.

**Step 4: Caching layer**

Posts and authors usually have skewed access (Pareto distribution). A request-scoped DataLoader caches within one request; a process-wide cache (with TTL and invalidation) catches the cross-request hits. For user data, even a 30-second cache pays for itself massively. Use Redis if you need cross-instance consistency, in-memory LRU if eventual is fine. Watch out for cache stampedes — use single-flight (`p-retry` / `singleflight` in Go) to coalesce concurrent misses.

**When denormalization IS the answer**

Denormalize when you have **read patterns the source schema can't serve efficiently no matter how clever the queries are**. Examples:

- Aggregate counts (post.commentCount) where computing on read is too expensive — store and maintain via triggers or event handlers.
- Read-mostly social graph data where fan-out is huge — Twitter-style timeline materialization.
- Cross-database joins your transactional store can't do.

For a `posts → comments → users` query on a single Postgres, you almost certainly don't need it. Get the indexes right, let DataLoader fan out separate batches, add field-aware projection, and measure. P99 should drop substantially without paying consistency tax.

```python
from collections import defaultdict

def group_by_first_letter(words: list[str]) -> dict[str, list[str]]:
    groups = defaultdict(list)
    for word in words:
        if word:  # skip empty strings
            groups[word[0].lower()].append(word)
    return dict(groups)

# Example
group_by_first_letter(['apple', 'banana', 'avocado', 'blueberry'])
# {'a': ['apple', 'avocado'], 'b': ['banana', 'blueberry']}
```

Right, proto3 is "everything is optional" by design — Google's lesson from proto2 was that `required` is a permanent decision you can't reverse without breaking old clients, and it caused more outages than it prevented. So the rollout pattern looks different.

The two-phase rollout for adding an enforced `tenant_id`:

```proto
// 1. Add the field as a regular optional field. Doesn't break any client.
message GetItemRequest {
  string sku = 1;
  string warehouse_id = 2;
  string tenant_id = 3;  // NEW, populated by all updated clients
}
```

Deploy the server with logging on the missing case, instrumented hard:

```go
func (s *Server) GetItem(ctx context.Context, req *pb.GetItemRequest) (*pb.Item, error) {
    if req.TenantId == "" {
        // Phase 1: log and accept, with a metric we can alert on
        missingTenantIdCounter.WithLabelValues(callerFromCtx(ctx)).Inc()
        // Fall back to legacy resolution from auth context
        req.TenantId = legacyTenantFromAuth(ctx)
    }
    // ...
}
```

Watch `missing_tenant_id_total` by caller. Drive each caller to zero by updating their generated client (now that the field is in the proto, it's a one-line code change). Once the metric is flat at zero for a few weeks, flip to reject:

```go
if req.TenantId == "" {
    return nil, status.Error(codes.InvalidArgument, "tenant_id is required")
}
```

For the schema-level enforcement and documentation, [protovalidate](https://github.com/bufbuild/protovalidate) is the modern answer:

```proto
import "buf/validate/validate.proto";

message GetItemRequest {
  string sku = 1 [(buf.validate.field).string.min_len = 1];
  string warehouse_id = 2 [(buf.validate.field).string.min_len = 1];
  string tenant_id = 3 [(buf.validate.field).string.min_len = 1];
}
```

That generates validators in every supported language and you call it as middleware. Now the requirement is in the proto contract, not just the server code.

External clients are the harder part — you can't just flip the switch. Standard playbook:

1. Add the field to the proto, ship to all clients via package update.
2. Announce deprecation of "tenant_id-less" requests in your changelog with a hard cutoff date (give external folks 90 days minimum).
3. Inject a `Deprecation: true` and `Sunset: <date>` header on responses where it's missing.
4. After the date, return INVALID_ARGUMENT.

Two anti-patterns to avoid:

- **Never delete the field or reuse the tag number.** If `tenant_id` ever becomes obsolete years later, mark it `deprecated = true` and `reserved 3;` — never reassign tag 3 to anything else, ever. Old clients with that tag in their on-disk data will misinterpret it.
- **Don't sneak the requirement in via server-side breakage.** Always go through the metric-driven rollout. "We added tenant_id and now Java callers throw NPE" is the kind of pager event that erodes trust in the platform team.
